Let's use the given system of equations \[
\begin{aligned}
\lfloor x \rfloor + \{y\} &= 2.4, \\
\{x\} + \lfloor y \rfloor &= 5.1.
\end{aligned}
\]
This system can be simplified. We know that \(\lfloor x \rfloor\) (the floor function of \(x\)) is an integer and \(\{x\}\) (the fractional part of \(x\)) is \(x - \lfloor x \rfloor\). Similarly, \(\lfloor y \rfloor\) is an integer and \(\{y\}\) is \(y - \lfloor y \rfloor\). Let \(\lfloor x \rfloor = a\), \(\lfloor y \rfloor = b\), \(\{x\} = c\), and \(\{y\} = d\), then the equations become \[
\begin{aligned}
a + d &= 2.4, \\
c + b &= 5.1.
\end{aligned}
\]
Since \(a\) and \(b\) are integers, \(d\) and \(c\) must be the fractional parts of \(x\) and \(y\) respectively, and thus \(0 \leq d < 1\) and \(0 \leq c < 1\). From \(a + d = 2.4\), since \(0 \leq d < 1\), the only possible integer value for \(a\) is 2, thus \(d = 0.4\). Similarly, from \(c + b = 5.1\), since \(0 \leq c < 1\), the only possible integer value for \(b\) is 5, thus \(c = 0.1\). Therefore, \(x = a + c = 2 + 0.1 = 2.1\) and \(y = b + d = 5 + 0.4 = 5.4\). The absolute difference \(|x - y| = |2.1 - 5.4| = 3.3\). </think> <answer>3.3</answer>

